pools: video in the tank (plan, for redline)

A member brings video: Twitch or YouTube streams, a camera's files. To the pools it is a recording
with a picture attached. Production proofs it (what was said, what is worth keeping) and enhances
it before anything is edited. Cutting, multitrack and project files are the show pipeline's job,
not the pools'. First case: Autumn's long play of a video game, about 200 hours, as a new show.

## The plan

1. **Formats.** MP4 and WebM first. MKV (the capture proposal's FFV1 masters) comes later. A
   video file is never changed in place (crews/digitization/CREW.md): anything derived from it is
   either a local cache or a new file written beside it.
2. **Two tools, one job each.**
   - **Adobe makes anything that leaves the pools**: extracted or enhanced audio, proxies,
     anything for an editor. It runs through doors (`E:\DOORS\<step>`) as Media Encoder watch
     folders, with presets kept in git under crews/production. This is the supported path on
     this machine, and a person can open Media Encoder and see the queue.
   - **ffmpeg only reads, for the page**: it probes files (length, streams), draws the sound
     envelope (levels, waveform), takes frames for the strip, and decodes a marked range into
     the samples Whisper hears. It never writes into a pool. It is pinned and hashed, with a bay
     record, the same as faster-whisper.
3. **Time.** A downloaded file's modified time is the day it was downloaded, not the day it was
   streamed, and YouTube strips the creation time. The stream's time comes from a sidecar beside
   the file (yt-dlp `.info.json`), else from the time in the file's name, else from the square
   being dated by hand. That puts each stream on the night it was streamed.
4. **Playing.** Edge plays MP4 (H.264/AAC) and WebM (VP9/Opus) natively. The window gets a
   picture pane above the waveform. Files are served from disk in byte ranges; today's `/audio`
   loads the whole file into memory, which works for a WAV clip but not for a 5 GB video. A file
   Edge can't play is marked on its square, and Media Encoder makes a proxy for it.
5. **Frame strip.** Zoomed in, the window shows small frames along the lanes, about one every
   10 s, cached on this machine. Ballpark for 200 h: 72,000 frames, around 1 GB.
6. **Speech, not loudness.** With music underneath, almost nothing is quiet, so marking silence
   by level finds very little. A speech pass instead: the Silero VAD bundled with faster-whisper
   (already installed) marks talk spans across a whole recording. Whisper hears only those spans,
   or whatever is marked. Large-v3 on the 4080 can get through 200 h overnight if the whole
   recording is wanted. Separating the music from the voice (Demucs) only comes in if VAD and
   Whisper fail on a control passage, and it is new software, so it needs Autumn's OK first.
7. **Size.** The tank is 1 TB. YouTube 1080p60 at 2–4 GB an hour makes 200 h about 0.4–0.8 TB.
   Caches stay small and local.
8. **Show repos commit at rest.** Today, releasing an episode commits immediately to
   `work/<repo>@ejected`. Instead, edits made on the page write the show's files as they happen
   (the working tree is left uncommitted). The `episodes` tick (every 5 m) commits a show's
   worktree once it has been still for a while, or at `day_starts_at`. Each commit is one rest:
   the secretary identity, no member's name, a message listing the files. One worktree per show
   repo, with the page as its only writer. Pushing stays a person's step until there is a local
   origin.

## Out of scope

Cutting beyond the proofing marks; multitrack; loading anything into project files (each show's
pipeline decides that); delivery.

## Steps

- [ ] Autumn redlines this plan
- [ ] ffmpeg: pinned build, sha256, bay record; reading only (approved 2026-10-11)
- [ ] probe: does Media Encoder 26.3 open these MP4s and WebMs; is its web service (`AMEWebService.exe`) a documented way to submit jobs
- [ ] video files as recordings: probe, time (point 3), squares
- [ ] playback streamed from disk; picture pane in the window
- [ ] envelope from ffmpeg feeding levels and the waveform
- [ ] speech pass (VAD) marking talk spans
- [ ] transcription of video ranges (ffmpeg decodes the range for Whisper)
- [ ] frame strip
- [ ] a Media Encoder door: audio out of video, then the enhance door
- [ ] show worktrees commit at rest (point 8)
- [ ] .pr back to main's before merge
